#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d604b35-b182-51ef-8ad8-153a353f0418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2e8c0b-8871-52a8-8f7e-27c536e84a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295618} {:answer/id #uuid "560da999-55c4-5b56-a1d6-481d32f3a37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295765} {:answer/id #uuid "a943da63-fb2b-5fdc-9bc3-6074acd881a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296157} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c83eab-72fe-5dac-9261-1a12e8b33cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d604b35-b182-51ef-8ad8-153a353f0418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a845396-80e0-59b7-bded-5ac38a7616cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6480ee2c-c900-5448-85b3-32f94538971e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288074" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $9v-5=4v+2$\n- $2v+3=2v+7$\n- $5v-1=5v-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9v-5 & =4v+2 \\\\ 9v-5-4v+5 & =4v+2-4v+5 \\\\ 5v & =7 \\\\ v & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $9v-5=4v+2$ has the unique solution $v=\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+3 & =2v+7 \\\\ 2v+3-2v & =2v+7-2v \\\\ 3 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $v.$ Therefore, the equation $2v+3=2v+7$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-1 & =5v-1 \\\\ 5v-1-5v & =5v-1-5v \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $v.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b3b264-c3bf-56ca-98eb-2d6cd21c3128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee88acd-7941-541d-92ae-327eb954fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295723} {:answer/id #uuid "920b99e8-cc75-5101-9bba-6a0d521d38ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296052} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6ea962-b6ce-5bb8-b5bf-25d51a764721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296184} {:answer/id #uuid "f2861c84-edf3-5ebd-851c-60ce99e270e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b3b264-c3bf-56ca-98eb-2d6cd21c3128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c2d36a-6f01-5415-a69e-1a9749544603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5426119-4ea0-5c84-bb6c-cf460c622db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-288425" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- $6x+1=6x+5$\n- $7x-4=6x-6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2cb23e-a320-550f-80fb-5d57177ced2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295443} {:answer/id #uuid "5a31613b-8925-5a47-a2df-6b3f46bb6e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785} {:answer/id #uuid "7935fd04-86fe-5c9d-b74f-227c3df9fdc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295929} {:answer/id #uuid "e40fb2df-e9bd-55aa-a47c-3fc4ba486d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296484} {:answer/id #uuid "ef187862-df62-575f-8ffd-89e5afb16587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a31613b-8925-5a47-a2df-6b3f46bb6e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df2639b-eb03-5fd2-8163-d1dd5acd6070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31ef6d07-7ebc-5067-b0a5-3f361bec8d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $2x-5=x-2$\n- $6x+1=6x+9$\n- $-4x+3=-4x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5987c83e-257d-55b4-a17e-3965dafe501e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295783} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd73961-4fa5-55a6-b016-4ed50daa78c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295946} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5b127e-bdcf-5063-975e-7d2cfc4e8774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9ae661-533a-5359-845d-1a9edbbbdc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296526} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6316b3-6aa1-5dae-ba84-4457b158e8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6316b3-6aa1-5dae-ba84-4457b158e8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3dc02a4-0b73-558c-b7e7-05f609d48e61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43eccfac-bd80-5f7b-abff-3250eee5dc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $3x+2=4x+1$\n- $2x-5=3x-3$\n- $5x-4=5x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064a647e-d06a-50bb-9cbc-0f17389eca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295298} {:answer/id #uuid "1066d92d-62c9-5dad-8b4c-dbbe71386d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3a0291-6ad9-5566-9fd5-0de08933b4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295630} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2ff4e9-ee34-51e6-b267-06c09d19013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1618392-44be-533c-81a8-efd84eeee3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nI. The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$\n\nIII. The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} {:answer/id #uuid "a4be644b-3c50-5bb5-b637-a1dc0590c6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303470} {:answer/id #uuid "be5348b7-6521-531c-9590-b40f2257782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303623} {:answer/id #uuid "bface693-37fe-568c-a4fa-08c4b3ba5757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303630} {:answer/id #uuid "e424eab9-c2e2-5989-8b94-f4410f99f5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3bff16-f60d-5153-a071-915cdea9c3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcddfdb-c38a-5f92-b718-28036cb5e0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302637} {:answer/id #uuid "24531df4-5849-5e8d-b717-57d07a8550b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302686} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15be87-c9e8-5225-a673-4b26b316be25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303004} {:answer/id #uuid "66137ed2-2b14-58d0-bd8a-ff3d608e1ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303074} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d947d0-6f8d-54b2-932e-06f1d5e06c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7603a78a-aca0-51e5-9c68-1571861f143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303180} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9253d0-497c-5ed8-9904-752fb5b447a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303413} {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} {:answer/id #uuid "b8164d95-12bd-5b59-aa2d-33ca3cd95e6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303598} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b02410-a499-5764-8c6c-bc4fea9102cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f51f02b-20a7-5356-a7bf-6883df31265e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7684d3-767a-52e5-bcc5-d780b7faa6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302753} {:answer/id #uuid "51c3b1e8-d753-5298-9a6f-e67e6cb118db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303225} {:answer/id #uuid "da2cbaaf-6e7b-5962-ac89-10a2621a85d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303814} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fad90-31aa-5644-9fc2-5632bf70c90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303225} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0510dd4-7330-5414-b48b-4c4f7e0d6ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb41985-a448-5edf-ac16-5140f9df994f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331727" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6ad6bd-ebba-5732-80c2-8d1f7fefd916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302649} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce96722-6380-533c-ba73-d1d4dba3de7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303023} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcb8921-abbb-595e-8400-563faaf3eedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} {:answer/id #uuid "b27edf92-4fc4-5f2b-8e2a-0406162eac16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303563} {:answer/id #uuid "ee01f996-8937-58e6-8682-6e1145ce7e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bcb8921-abbb-595e-8400-563faaf3eedc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4ac981-a1ea-5619-b6b7-e06c26ba211c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f3dd1b-d16f-5aa1-9eba-10b4e0fd71e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2459a5ff-007e-530b-8edd-f46a552247c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4a8220-7054-525a-8afa-9194364f34cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303014} {:answer/id #uuid "86c7d469-f99c-5c11-aeb9-bf421146bc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303299} {:answer/id #uuid "923a9172-9e8c-54b3-b41b-41a15e5952e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303364} {:answer/id #uuid "efe9d738-6b6f-52a9-a41b-35b9bd06d1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2459a5ff-007e-530b-8edd-f46a552247c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} :answer-field/id #uuid "c46c2ddf-b5c1-516e-be0e-d85055c76fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8cfe21c-18ac-5f6d-9859-3a5cbe483542" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}+7xy=5$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0166a0e9-e475-5c8f-b032-9082b7fcde2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302458} {:answer/id #uuid "5314567f-e5df-5ca2-80e8-15b33f649506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302971} {:answer/id #uuid "539ad669-6292-539a-9593-3f19bceb9427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302976} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f64220-da24-5e82-8ca4-f2181aec3314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303555} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd819bc-3592-58f6-8e43-71f8e2d6858e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd819bc-3592-58f6-8e43-71f8e2d6858e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303932} :answer-field/id #uuid "813a5976-379b-5f02-85ae-eb526a514659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e88e764-3128-53c6-9d2a-d1f58dae12a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085f0491-01b7-5bf1-9493-4de4eb3adc20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302513} {:answer/id #uuid "53fae90d-e1e5-5e33-bf40-bc28be9e851a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302978} {:answer/id #uuid "9e34058f-f878-5f33-8037-f52171e96c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303441} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dc1111-b6e1-50ea-8948-d3e287173f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303980} {:answer/id #uuid "f4be8e3e-d3ca-5fac-a0d8-1f76a3eab20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fae90d-e1e5-5e33-bf40-bc28be9e851a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302978} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ba6d73-52f7-5082-b9b0-19271c2e615c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b9025a-0c2a-511c-b23e-f617ee7031f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331739" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0346d22e-99ca-51c0-8e3c-59b50a525a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} {:answer/id #uuid "1832ec2d-46da-5208-9b06-d9f7868bef40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302616} {:answer/id #uuid "97ace580-565a-598e-9cb1-40493c663d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303399} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a0b3a0-bdcf-5820-a95c-e2146d10bb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303793} {:answer/id #uuid "db480e54-f880-5865-b053-131cc54e1a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0346d22e-99ca-51c0-8e3c-59b50a525a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5d3bec-6e2b-5dc7-98fa-15f9ea28173f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca0875e1-4252-5a69-bfc7-c43bf6770e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3527a389-4846-52d0-a26e-ab7610b3c975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302786} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae9285b-64e1-54ff-a6e6-ae3a2dd4ef18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302929} {:answer/id #uuid "9079b25c-a77d-5d40-ad19-55fe4a678350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303359} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d4ad9a-fb93-5bbb-be30-9effc80dc1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303554} {:answer/id #uuid "e124e1e6-0f1a-55b2-8382-60e9817db497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e124e1e6-0f1a-55b2-8382-60e9817db497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303860} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec98d2f-0402-5a20-8b48-beacf10636a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26251e42-10da-5a5f-8853-15a03bb82549" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59011896-39e7-52bf-8261-a97745a7ea6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321848} {:answer/id #uuid "9083ca4f-1840-5d1f-bbd0-a3aebcfb6ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322293} {:answer/id #uuid "947fb86d-b16d-5151-bc45-bfff1a5ca857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322327} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d93329-76ba-546c-ae25-7c267f4496a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322815} {:answer/id #uuid "e83b1617-67a3-5666-a040-d549cf9c83ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9083ca4f-1840-5d1f-bbd0-a3aebcfb6ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322293} :answer-field/id #uuid "a08cc063-fbcd-53b1-b1a1-f8bf94126e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69ab5c8f-74d2-578a-ab14-a3f834908b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36002" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c51c5a4de562e5a3301ea76afc6af57b459acd4c46e5c65d54afc226d883a137.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,0]$\n- $x\\in (-3,1)$\n- $x\\in [0,3)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,0]$ and $x\\in [0,3).$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/00/00a80dde24ca012e0282362224c8b63764f5e149a8bfcda9c76ff4eb958fa93a.png)\n\n \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,1)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea54918e2ef4310b58e9cf8a0c9b1f9757a404d97dbbfd9e5b037af2193f38c.png)\n\n \n\nHence, the correct answer is 'I and III only''."}]}
